{
  "description": "The forms with nasal harmony that Walker (2000) gives for Sundanese, the Johore dialect of Malay and the Kolokuma dialect of Ijo, examples (3) to (5), as printed: a tilde marks a nasalized segment, the glottals included. The forms of Scottish Gaelic and Tuyuca are not entered.",
  "Types": {
    "Language": [
      {
        "Value": "Sundanese",
        "Lean": "sundanese",
        "Description": "Sundanese, where nasality spreads rightward from a nasal stop through vowels and glottals"
      },
      {
        "Value": "Malay (Johore dialect)",
        "Lean": "malay",
        "Description": "the Johore dialect of Malay, where it spreads rightward from a nasal stop through glides as well"
      },
      {
        "Value": "Ijo (Kolokuma dialect)",
        "Lean": "ijo",
        "Description": "the Kolokuma dialect of Ijo, where it spreads leftward from a nasal consonant or vowel, through liquids as well"
      }
    ]
  },
